# Alloy Calculator Opened-Container Transfers

Source proposal: the conversation evaluation of extending alloy-calculator deposits to containers currently opened by the player.

## Goal and boundaries

Allow ingredient and fuel deposits to withdraw from, and return incorrect or excess items to, eligible opened containers alongside the player's backpack and hotbar. Preserve existing metal matching, fuel-temperature selection, quantity calculation, and vanilla client-predicted transfer packets.

This plan does not add proximity searches, open containers automatically, introduce packets, make deposits atomic, or change alloy calculations. Partial completion before a later transfer failure remains possible; successful movements must remain accounted for.

## Assumptions and decisions to resolve

    ☑ Establish which opened inventories qualify using installed engine/source contracts before coding selection.
        ☑ Recommended initial scope: ordinary storage containers; exclude the target firepit, unrelated player inventories, creative inventories, and other processing inventories.
        ☑ Inspect storage inventory classes and existing container owning APIs; do not assume InventoryGeneric alone identifies ordinary storage, or introduce a broad type/name heuristic without evidence.
        ☑ Record support for vanilla storage variants and the boundary for modded storage; document any unsupported cases explicitly.
    ☑ Confirm transfer priority, using these recommended defaults unless the user changes them:
        ☑ Withdraw from smallest matching stacks first across the whole pool; break ties by backpack, hotbar, then stable opened-container order.
        ☑ Return to compatible existing stacks before empty slots; within each group prefer backpack, hotbar, then stable opened-container order.
        ☑ Do not track original provenance or promise return to the original container.

Resolved implementation decisions (2026-10-07):

- Initial storage eligibility resolves InventoryBase.Pos through the world's block accessor, accepts BlockEntityGenericContainer, BlockEntityGenericTypedContainer (including labeled chests), or BlockEntityCrate owners, and requires reference identity with the owner's current Inventory. The inventory must appear in OpenedInventories. This supports generic chests/baskets and typed storage variants; crates qualify only when their inventory is actually opened. Closed crates are not opened automatically.
- Modded subclasses of these storage owners inherit eligibility and retain their slot restrictions. Other modded storage owners, entity-attached storage, racks, liquid-only owners, and processing owners are outside the initial support boundary. InventoryGeneric alone and inventory class-name strings do not establish eligibility.
- Pool order is backpack, hotbar, then the current OpenedInventories list order. Withdrawal consumers retain stable smallest-stack-first ordering; returns retain existing-stack-first ordering with pool order as the tie breaker. InventoriesOrdered is not used to broaden eligibility or enumerate unopened inventories. No source provenance is retained.
- Phase 1 provides selection and live directional probes only; services remain unchanged until their integration tasks. Slot collection includes empty and locked slots so source and destination eligibility can be evaluated separately at use time.
- Source contracts: D:/CODE/VintageStory/vssurvivalmod/BlockEntity/{BEContainer,BEGenericContainer,BEGenericTypedContainer,BELabeledChest,BECrate}.cs; D:/CODE/VintageStory/vsapi/Common/Entity/Player/IPlayerInventoryManager.cs and D:/CODE/VintageStory/vsapi/Common/Inventory/{IInventory,InventoryBase,ItemSlot}.cs. Existing AutoStashing container targets, crate policy, planner, and transfer executor supply reuse-boundary evidence, not calculator policy.

## Phase 1: Establish the eligible inventory pool

Outcome: ingredient and fuel services can obtain one consistently ordered, duplicate-free pool without changing deposit behavior yet.

    ☑ Inventory reusable APIs and implementation boundaries.
        ☑ Inspect IPlayerInventoryManager.OpenedInventories, InventoriesOrdered, ItemSlot.CanTake, CanTakeFrom, CanHold, and inventory CanContain contracts.
        ☑ Inspect specialized container slot/transfer behavior and the existing AutoStashing policies for reusable contracts; avoid coupling calculator logic to unrelated stash policy.
        ☑ Resolve the storage eligibility and ordering assumptions above and record the concrete rule here.
    ☑ Add the narrowly responsible AlloyTransferInventoryPolicy component under src/AlloyCalculator.
        ☑ Preserve backpack/hotbar selection and existing missing-inventory error behavior.
        ☑ Append only eligible inventories currently in OpenedInventories, excluding the entire target firepit inventory.
        ☑ Deduplicate inventory and slot identities so availability cannot be counted twice and a slot cannot transfer to itself.
        ☑ Keep source and destination eligibility directional: take restrictions govern withdrawal; put restrictions govern returns.
        ☑ Obtain fresh eligible inventories for each planning/execution operation; do not keep an inventory cache across clicks.
        ☑ Add XML documentation and logical-process comments; organize methods by visibility and responsibility.
    ☑ Verify pool ordering, duplicate exclusion, target exclusion, storage eligibility, and closed/unrelated inventory exclusion with focused unit tests.

Completion criteria:

    ☑ Eligibility has an evidenced engine contract and an explicit supported-storage boundary.
    ☑ Pool tests demonstrate stable ordering and no duplicate availability.
    ☑ Existing player-only behavior remains the default when no qualifying container is open.

## Phase 2: Integrate ingredient deposits and restricted transfers

Outcome: ingredient planning and corrections operate across the expanded pool with consistent transfer restrictions.

    ☑ Replace backpack/hotbar assembly in AlloyDepositService.CreatePlan and ExecutePlan with the shared selection component.
        ☑ Preserve separate counting of existing target cooking-slot contents; never add the target inventory to the external pool.
        ☑ Count external matching items only when withdrawable under applicable source-slot rules.
        ☑ Re-evaluate availability and currently opened inventories at execution rather than retaining stale container references from planning.
    ☑ Update ClientInventorySlotReconciler for the expanded pool.
        ☑ Rename playerSlots parameters to reflect their broader responsibility.
        ☑ Preserve smallest-stack-first withdrawal with documented tie ordering.
        ☑ Check applicable source and destination slot/inventory restrictions, including CanContain, before attempting transfers; continue using TryTransferTo and actual MovedQuantity as authoritative results.
        ☑ Preserve existing-stack-first returns and documented tie ordering without bypassing specialized slot behavior.
        ☑ If an external container closes during correction, stop using its slots; preserve completed transfers and report any remaining deficit/space failure through existing result codes.
        ☑ Preserve the requirement that the target firepit remains open; establish where to recheck it before subsequent movements if an operation callback changes inventory state.
        ☑ Do not add rollback or broad capacity simulation as part of this change.
    ☑ Add focused ingredient/reconciler tests.
        ☑ Ingredients available only in an opened container and split across player/container inventories.
        ☑ Incorrect/excess metals returned to an opened container when player inventories are full.
        ☑ Locked or specialized source/destination slots, incompatible contents, and exhausted return capacity.
        ☑ Container closed between planning and execution, and closure during a transfer callback.
        ☑ Duplicate references and target-firepit contents cannot inflate availability or create self-transfers.
        ☑ Priority, quantities, item conservation, packets, and partial-failure accounting remain correct.

Completion criteria:

    ☑ Both planning and execution use the same pool policy and current opened-container state.
    ☑ Restricted items are not counted as freely available, and invalid destinations are not used.
    ☑ Focused ingredient and reconciler tests pass without changing metal matching or slot allocation.

## Phase 3: Integrate fuel deposits

Outcome: fuel selection and correction use the same opened-container pool as ingredients.

    ☑ Replace backpack/hotbar assembly in AlloyFuelDepositService.CreatePlan and ExecutePlan with shared selection.
        ☑ Include suitable, withdrawable container fuel in candidate quantities.
        ☑ Continue accounting separately for queued fuel; preserve the expected-current-fuel guard.
        ☑ Preserve positive burn-duration requirements, effective burn-temperature eligibility, heat/burn-duration modifiers, minimum required-fuel selection, and stack-size limits.
        ☑ Permit incompatible/excess queued fuel to return to eligible containers through the same reconciler.
    ☑ Verify that GuiDialogAlloyCalculator continues to run ingredient correction followed by fuel correction using current inventory state; avoid moving inventory policy into the GUI.
    ☑ Add focused fuel tests for container-only fuel, combined availability, replacement/excess returns, locked sources, closure after planning, and too-cold container fuel.

Completion criteria:

    ☑ Ingredient and fuel deposits share eligibility and transfer-order rules.
    ☑ Existing fuel-temperature and fuel-quantity regressions continue to pass.
    ☑ Closed/restricted container fuel does not yield a successful unavailable-fuel plan.

## Phase 4: Validation and acceptance

Outcome: implementation is reviewable with automated receipts and clearly separated live-runtime acceptance.

    ☐ Run build/test tools through subagents as required by repository instructions.
        ☐ Run the AlloyCalculator unit suite, including new inventory-policy, service, and reconciler cases.
        ☐ Run additional affected tests only if shared code beyond AlloyCalculator changed.
        ☐ Record the command, result counts, and material warnings in the completion report; avoid adding unsolicited receipt files.
    ☐ Review the implementation against this complete plan and the source proposal a second time.
        ☐ Verify supported-container coverage, source/destination restrictions, current-open-state checks, packet authority, conservation, ordering, and preserved partial-failure semantics.
        ☐ Obtain an independent completion audit before checking completion markers; do not treat unexecuted live checks as passed.
    ☐ Request user-run live acceptance without launching the game automatically.
        ☐ Deposit metals and fuel from an opened supported storage container.
        ☐ Return incorrect/excess items to storage with full player inventories.
        ☐ Confirm client/server inventory agreement and normal behavior after the container closes.
    ☐ Summarize supported containers, priority, partial-failure behavior, automated results, and any outstanding live acceptance.

Final acceptance:

    ☐ Every source-proposal requirement maps to implemented behavior or an explicit approved deferral.
    ☐ Required automated validation, second review, and independent audit are complete.
    ☐ Live acceptance is complete or clearly recorded as outstanding; headless tests do not establish multiplayer/runtime acceptance.
